    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nXiaomi Appears To Have Run Out of Steam, Despite Promising SkyNomad Sales in October\n89 photos\nPhoto: Xiaomi\nXiaomi prepares to end 2026 with a mixed bag of achievements and is likely to miss its self-imposed sales target despite encouraging SkyNomad sales. This showcases the challenges of staying ahead in the tough Chinese market, where new models have a shelf life of less than a year. Despite a recent refresh, the SU7 sales have stalled, and the YU7 has been dragging its feet for a while, so SkyNomad's success might not last long.\nXiaomi's story is one of the most impressive in the automotive industry. The technology company, known just a few years ago for its phones and IoT devices, established itself as a carmaker and rose to the top of car sales in China. This is no small feat, especially considering the highly competitive Chinese market. The mighty Apple tried to make a car before Xiaomi, and broke its teeth after burning over $10 billion.\nEvery single model Xiaomi launched has been a bestseller in its segment, at least for a period. The SU7 quickly moved past the Tesla Model 3 in the sales charts, and it looked unstoppable. The YU7 had some good months surpassing the Tesla Model Y, although that didn't last long. As soon as Xiaomi released the refreshed SU7 early this year, the YU7's sales stalled. At the same time, orders for the electric sedan spiked.\nXiaomi's SkyNomad brand reported encouraging sales in October The same story repeated with Xiaomi's new car brand SkyNomad. Launched with great fanfare on September 7 in China, SkyNomad quickly became the public's favorite. The brand focuses on range-extended SUVs, unlike Xiaomi's core models SU7 and YU7, which are exclusively offered as battery-electric vehicles.\nThe first SkyNomad models, the N70 and N90, accounted for over 54% of Xiaomi's sales in October, the first full calendar month on sale since launch. The former sold 14,558 units, while the larger and more expensive N90 contributed 12,589 units to the tally. Xiaomi previously reported over 70,000 firm orders for the SkyNomad vehicles.\nPhoto: Xiaomi Auto SkyNomad's numbers look even more impressive considering the overall market slump in October. Passenger vehicle sales fell 27% in October, while the new energy segment (which combines BEVs, EREVs, and PHEVs) dropped 13.7%. This marks the tenth consecutive month of decline, helping SkyNomad's results shine even brighter.\nXiaomi's core EV models have already become obsolete Xiaomi reported total sales of 50,168 vehicles in October, a 25.4% gain from September. However, sales of the SU7 sedan, which was recently refreshed, fell 17.6% to 12,984 units, while the YU7 dropped 15.1% to 10,037. This shows that SkyNomad vehicles were the only bright spot in Xiaomi's October sales report.\nThis is not unusual for a market that always cherishes the latest and greatest, with little regard for yesterday's champions. In the public's view, the SU7 and YU7 are already old cars, especially as competitor carmakers have been churning new models every month. There are only so many car buyers, and that race to get the newest toy has created unique challenges for carmakers in China.\nXiaomi has done well while it was the new kid on the block in 2024 and 2025, but this year, only the SkyNomad models have excited buyers. The shelf life of new car models in China is very short, forcing carmakers to make incredible efforts to bring new models without affecting profitability. This probably explains why carmakers have so many models that appear to overlap, or at least lack any distinctive features.\nXiaomi will likely miss the 2026 sales target, but what can it do? The SkyNomad brand might look successful now, but six months down the road, it might share Xiaomi SU7 and YU7's fate as the novelty wears out. Absent new model launches, Xiaomi might find itself slipping to the bottom of the Chinese sales charts. As fewer cars are bought, profitability suffers, and at some point, it will be forced to make a decision.\nPhoto: Xiaomi Auto Xiaomi has guided for 550,000 vehicles sold in 2026, and it appears to be on track to miss this target. During the January-October period, the company sold a total of 336,658 vehicles, which represents about 61% of its annual target. Reaching the 550,000-unit mark would require selling an average of over 106,000 vehicles in each of the final two months. That's more than double the October volume. Even with the year-end sales surge in China, that is a difficult task.\nSince this is not the first time a Chinese carmaker (or, indeed, any carmaker operating in China) has hit a snag with local sales, it's highly likely that Xiaomi is already planning its next moves. The most obvious, which was announced a while back, is expanding to new markets. Xiaomi plans to start selling its EVs in Europe. Sales are expected to debut in early 2027 with the SU7 sedan.\nThis should provide a respite and offer Xiaomi some room to breathe, while also improving the profitability of its production lines. With healthy sales propped up by an anticipated European success, Xiaomi can continue investing in new vehicle lines at home, keeping the wheel spinning. This is the second move, expanding the vehicle lineup so that buyers stay hooked on the company's offers.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nxiaomi skynomad skynomad sales xiaomi sales\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT New-Gen Feature Coming to Apple Maps, Making ETAs More Accurate Than in Google Maps",
